WEBVTT

00:00:00.000 --> 00:00:10.320
The natural world is full of extraordinary animals with amazing life histories.

00:00:10.320 --> 00:00:16.400
Yet certain stories are more intriguing than most.

00:00:16.400 --> 00:00:23.640
The mysteries of a butterfly's life cycle or the strange biology of the emperor penguin.

00:00:23.640 --> 00:00:31.000
Some of these creatures were surrounded by myth and misunderstandings for a very long time.

00:00:31.000 --> 00:00:35.640
And some have only recently revealed their secrets.

00:00:35.640 --> 00:00:39.240
These are the animals that stand out from the crowd.

00:00:39.240 --> 00:00:53.400
The curiosities I find particularly fascinating.

00:00:53.400 --> 00:01:02.440
Salamanders can regenerate entire legs and tails to replace ones that they lose.

00:01:02.440 --> 00:01:09.240
And moose can regrow their enormous antlers every year.

00:01:09.240 --> 00:01:22.440
How do these animals regenerate entire body parts and why isn't it possible for all animals to do the same?

00:01:22.440 --> 00:01:28.120
When I was a boy, my father gave me one of these for my eighth birthday.

00:01:28.120 --> 00:01:30.840
It's a fire salamander.

00:01:30.840 --> 00:01:35.160
They may look like lizards, but in fact they're not reptiles.

00:01:35.160 --> 00:01:38.680
They're amphibians with moist skins.

00:01:38.680 --> 00:01:42.680
For centuries, mythical stories surrounded these creatures.

00:01:42.680 --> 00:01:49.840
It was believed that they were icy cold animals that could dwell within fires unharmed by the heat.

00:01:49.840 --> 00:02:00.720
Although their fire surviving powers may be untrue, the salamander nonetheless possesses a real natural ability that is just as extraordinary.

00:02:00.720 --> 00:02:13.120
They're able to regrow damage, tails, legs and other parts of the body through a process called regeneration.

00:02:13.120 --> 00:02:18.000
There are more than 600 different species of salamander.

00:02:18.000 --> 00:02:33.520
They range in size from just a couple of centimeters up to the world's largest amphibian, the Chinese giant salamander, that can grow to over a meter and a half in length.

00:02:33.520 --> 00:02:45.600
Salamanders are predators and many hunt for small invertebrates such as slugs and worms.

00:02:45.600 --> 00:02:54.160
But sometimes they hunt each other with dramatic consequences.

00:02:54.160 --> 00:03:11.040
This tiny North American redback salamander is on the menu of the much bigger seal salamander.

00:03:11.040 --> 00:03:17.200
Time to make a retreat.

00:03:17.200 --> 00:03:22.240
This may look shocking, but the redback isn't badly injured.

00:03:22.240 --> 00:03:30.720
A weak point in its skin allows its tail to break off easily.

00:03:30.720 --> 00:03:38.000
Incredibly, it will regrow a new tail in just a matter of weeks.

00:03:38.000 --> 00:03:47.120
This ability to replace an entire body part is unusual among adult vertebrates and seems almost magical.

00:03:47.120 --> 00:03:51.520
Regeneration is a subject that fascinates us.

00:03:51.520 --> 00:03:58.400
Mott and Mensen has spent a lot of money and time studying the ways our own bodies can regenerate tissue.

00:03:58.400 --> 00:04:07.680
All living creatures, including humans, have the ability to repair damaged parts of the body, but the extent of that repair varies considerably.

00:04:07.680 --> 00:04:16.160
A small instance, we have the ability to regrow the tips of our fingers if they're severed, but we lose this ability as we age.

00:04:16.160 --> 00:04:26.880
So animals like salamanders, with their super regenerative powers, seem intriguing to us.

00:04:26.880 --> 00:04:36.320
Regeneration had been known about since ancient times, but for a long time no one understood how it happened.

00:04:36.320 --> 00:04:42.960
In the 17th and 18th century, there was a new wave of scientific discovery.

00:04:42.960 --> 00:04:58.400
A brilliant Italian scientist named Lazzarano Spallanzani made meticulous observations into regeneration across many different species and shared his ideas in detailed letters.

00:04:58.400 --> 00:05:10.400
In November 1765, he wrote to the eminent scientist Charles Bonnet, whom he regularly corresponded with, to announce that he had discovered tail regeneration in salamanders.

00:05:10.400 --> 00:05:20.480
Throughout the following year, he followed up his initial observations with numerous experiments to try to understand how the salamander could regrow a tail, just like the original.

00:05:20.480 --> 00:05:34.560
He found that all species of salamander that he tested could regrow their tails when injured, and they did so more rapidly in summer than in winter, and retained this incredible ability throughout their lives.

00:05:34.560 --> 00:05:38.400
Spallanzani advocated a radical theory.

00:05:38.400 --> 00:05:51.440
He thought that salamanders already possessed a number of miniature spare parts at the base of each limb that could grow in size to replace a lost or damaged one.

00:05:51.440 --> 00:05:55.680
He was unable to prove this theory, but he didn't give up.

00:05:55.680 --> 00:06:02.160
He studied salamander tadpoles and came up with another, even more interesting idea.

00:06:02.160 --> 00:06:12.800
A year after his initial letter, Spallanzani once again wrote to Charles Bonnet, this time with detailed descriptions of further experiments into tail regeneration.

00:06:12.800 --> 00:06:27.280
Most notably, in this description, he wrote, I am almost led to believe that the tail regenerates in tadpoles are more of an elongation of the old parts than a development from a germ.

00:06:27.280 --> 00:06:41.840
This suggests that Spallanzani was on the right track, but the idea that the salamander could regrow a new tail from seemingly nothing was not well supported, and Spallanzani was therefore never willing to pursue the idea further.

00:06:41.840 --> 00:06:53.680
However, there's no doubt that his research helped to lead other scientists closer towards proving what really happens when a salamander regrows its tail.

00:06:53.680 --> 00:07:07.200
In fact, Spallanzani's rough sketches did make sense, and they were the first to describe some of the vital processes in the remarkable growth of new limbs that we understand better today.

00:07:07.200 --> 00:07:15.040
When a limb is lost, the exposed blood vessels and tissue contract to quickly stop any bleeding.

00:07:15.040 --> 00:07:23.680
Then skin from the edges begins to grow across the damaged area to protect the body from infection.

00:07:23.680 --> 00:07:33.280
Now, cells that were once dormant begin dividing and multiplying to create new ones.

00:07:33.280 --> 00:07:44.160
Each cell retains the kind of memory of the type of tissue it used to be, so a new cell that regrows from damaged muscle will always become muscle.

00:07:44.160 --> 00:07:51.120
Within weeks, the salamander has a full-grown leg almost identical to the original.

00:07:51.120 --> 00:07:59.840
Although we now know the steps that take place during the regeneration of body parts, we still don't fully understand what triggers this kind of response.

00:07:59.840 --> 00:08:06.000
But it seems the answer may lie in how the salamander's body responds to injury.

00:08:06.000 --> 00:08:12.720
In humans, if an arm is severed, the cells die, alerting the immune system to the problem.

00:08:12.720 --> 00:08:21.120
In response, the area becomes swollen and is covered over with scar tissue, preventing any new growth occurring.

00:08:21.120 --> 00:08:32.480
But in salamanders, the immune system responds differently, and instead of forming a scar, it triggers regeneration.

00:08:32.480 --> 00:08:42.080
Another rather unusual-looking salamander that lives in the fresh waters of Mexico sheds new light on how this happens.

00:08:42.080 --> 00:08:51.760
Axolotls are among the best regenerators in the natural world, and scientists wondered if their blood played a role in the process.

00:08:51.760 --> 00:09:01.760
Like us, they have special white blood cells that consume invading bacteria and damage tissue around injuries and wounds.

00:09:01.760 --> 00:09:11.440
Researchers removed them, and the results were surprising. The axolotl was unable to regrow new limbs.

00:09:11.440 --> 00:09:17.120
So white blood cells were part of the secret of their powers of regeneration.

00:09:17.120 --> 00:09:29.280
Understanding the role of the salamander's blood cells in regrowing limbs could be a step towards discovering why they can regenerate body parts and we can't.

00:09:29.280 --> 00:09:36.160
All amphibians have tadpoles which develop limbs and enable them to move onto land.

00:09:36.160 --> 00:09:41.920
But salamanders are able to re-trigger that remarkable process.

00:09:41.920 --> 00:09:46.080
We too undergo extraordinary development in the womb.

00:09:46.080 --> 00:09:54.240
Maybe, like the salamander, there is a way of us retaining this ability into our adult lives as well.

00:09:54.240 --> 00:10:03.760
The salamander has a truly amazing ability to regrow complex body parts to enhance its chances of survival.

00:10:03.760 --> 00:10:16.960
While we don't yet know all the answers, it's likely that this incredible creature could revolutionise modern medicine and the way we treat injuries.

00:10:16.960 --> 00:10:37.920
Next, we uncover the secret behind how the moose regrows its enormous new antlers every year and discover what happens when regeneration goes wrong.

00:10:37.920 --> 00:10:43.520
The salamander's ability to regrow entire limbs is truly remarkable.

00:10:43.520 --> 00:10:53.440
But moose and other deer possess huge antlers that are shed and regrown each season.

00:10:53.440 --> 00:11:07.440
How can they regenerate such large body parts every single year?

00:11:07.440 --> 00:11:13.520
This impressive skeleton belonged to one of the biggest deer to ever live on the planet.

00:11:13.520 --> 00:11:15.680
It's an Irish elk.

00:11:15.680 --> 00:11:22.480
Its antlers are enormous. They're almost four metres, 12 feet across, and they weigh 40 kilos.

00:11:22.480 --> 00:11:33.680
An Irishman named Dr Molyneux first scientifically described the elk in 1697 from specimens taken out of an Irish peat bog.

00:11:33.680 --> 00:11:42.080
Some believed that this elk was a large moose and were convinced living specimens could be found elsewhere across Europe and Russia.

00:11:42.080 --> 00:11:52.320
But not everyone agreed, and a debate about the life of this creature would continue for more than 100 years.

00:11:52.320 --> 00:12:01.200
The skeleton of an Irish elk looks very similar to that of a moose.

00:12:01.200 --> 00:12:07.120
So it's easy to see why many believe them to be the same animal.

00:12:07.120 --> 00:12:12.960
Both have very impressive antlers.

00:12:12.960 --> 00:12:19.120
Antlers are only found in the deer family and are made of bone.

00:12:19.120 --> 00:12:26.480
Unlike horns, which are permanent structures, they are shed and replaced every year.

00:12:26.480 --> 00:12:30.560
But how can deer regrow huge chunks of bone?

00:12:30.560 --> 00:12:35.840
Something no other mammal can do.

00:12:35.840 --> 00:12:43.360
Moose, like this young bull behind me, start growing their new antlers immediately after they shed their old ones.

00:12:43.360 --> 00:12:52.960
The antlers first appear on little bumps by the side of the head, known as pedicles, and they have a soft furry covering called velvet.

00:12:52.960 --> 00:12:58.880
This is vital to their amazing powers of regeneration.

00:12:58.880 --> 00:13:06.960
Blood vessels at the base start the growth, but as the antler gets longer, this blood supply is cut off.

00:13:06.960 --> 00:13:17.360
Then blood vessels within the velvet take over and transport nutrients and growth hormones to the growing tips.

00:13:17.360 --> 00:13:31.760
In older males, the antlers can grow at a rate of two centimeters a day, making it the fastest growing bone of any animal.

00:13:31.760 --> 00:13:36.720
Once at full size, the velvet is shed.

00:13:36.720 --> 00:13:48.400
The animal rubs its head against a tree to encourage the thin velvet to fall off.

00:13:48.400 --> 00:13:59.600
It may look gruesome, but it's a natural part of the animal cycle and does the animal no harm.

00:13:59.600 --> 00:14:05.120
But why should a huge set of antlers be regrown every year?

00:14:05.120 --> 00:14:08.800
It's a question that baffled early naturalists.

00:14:08.800 --> 00:14:16.560
Until Charles Darwin suggested it may be to do with attracting the opposite sex.

00:14:16.560 --> 00:14:24.960
In the first few years of adulthood, the antlers are small, and as a result, young males remain subordinate to the larger bulls.

00:14:24.960 --> 00:14:43.520
But as they get older and their body size increases, so the antlers will also increase, eventually becoming impressive ornaments with which to compete for females.

00:14:43.520 --> 00:14:48.960
Those with the biggest antlers are certainly more attractive to the females.

00:14:48.960 --> 00:14:55.440
Maybe they are an indicator of fitness and strength.

00:14:55.440 --> 00:15:09.360
And it's no coincidence that antlers are at their full size during the breeding season.

00:15:09.360 --> 00:15:21.520
This is a time when a bull must protect his harem and see off competitors.

00:15:21.520 --> 00:15:30.960
Competing males tilt their heads to show off their antlers to their best advantage.

00:15:30.960 --> 00:15:53.760
But if the bulls are equally matched, then the competitors fight.

00:15:53.760 --> 00:16:00.560
The winner then gains access to the females.

00:16:00.560 --> 00:16:04.240
The benefits of such a victory are huge.

00:16:04.240 --> 00:16:12.480
But to get to that point, every young bull must for many years grow and regrow antlers.

00:16:12.480 --> 00:16:18.240
It's a big investment draining the body of vital resources.

00:16:18.240 --> 00:16:24.000
And no investment was bigger than that of the Irish elk.

00:16:24.000 --> 00:16:31.360
The sheer size of these antlers had led some to argue that they were unlikely to have been used in physical combat.

00:16:31.360 --> 00:16:38.160
Unlike other deer, the antlers of the Irish elk grew with a large, flat, palm-like plane facing forwards.

00:16:38.160 --> 00:16:44.080
So that if a bull looked straight ahead, it would be at its biggest and most impressive.

00:16:44.080 --> 00:16:53.040
In this way, they may have been able to intimidate rivals and attract females without actually fighting.

00:16:53.040 --> 00:17:01.520
So although the Irish elk was armed with what appear to be enormous weapons, it seems they were mostly for show.

00:17:01.520 --> 00:17:07.360
But this strategy may have been an advantage for the large elk.

00:17:07.360 --> 00:17:15.920
Fighting is always a risky business and will often result in serious injuries.

00:17:15.920 --> 00:17:20.320
After the greening season, the antlers are discarded.

00:17:20.320 --> 00:17:26.080
Moose shed theirs in the winter, whereas smaller deer keep theirs until the next spring.

00:17:26.080 --> 00:17:34.480
This may be because the moose antlers are such a heavy load to carry throughout the winter.

00:17:34.480 --> 00:17:37.200
But why are antlers shed at all?

00:17:37.200 --> 00:17:41.840
Antlers are made of dead bone and can't be repaired.

00:17:41.840 --> 00:17:49.760
If a moose damages an antler during a fight, it will lose its chance of mating for that season.

00:17:49.760 --> 00:17:59.120
By shedding and regrowing their antlers each year, bulls ensure that they stay in the mating game.

00:17:59.120 --> 00:18:04.800
Just before antlers are shed, minerals within them are reabsorbed from the base,

00:18:04.800 --> 00:18:11.600
weakening the structure so that they eventually fall off.

00:18:11.600 --> 00:18:19.360
The flesh underneath is exposed, but not for long, as new skin soon covers the wound.

00:18:19.360 --> 00:18:24.240
Experiments have shown that the skin lesion that forms over the open wound

00:18:24.240 --> 00:18:31.360
creates a connection with the underlying tissue that is crucial to regeneration.

00:18:31.360 --> 00:18:36.320
If this connection isn't made, the production of velvet will be interrupted

00:18:36.320 --> 00:18:45.920
and the antlers will either not grow at all or develop into strange shapes.

00:18:45.920 --> 00:18:48.880
So what about the Irish elk?

00:18:48.880 --> 00:18:55.600
Could the problems of regenerating such gigantic antlers have determined its fate?

00:18:55.600 --> 00:19:00.400
The French scientist Georges Cuvier was keen to demonstrate that the Irish elk

00:19:00.400 --> 00:19:04.720
was a unique species that had become extinct.

00:19:04.720 --> 00:19:10.720
To prove his point, Cuvier undertook a detailed examination of Irish elk fossils.

00:19:10.720 --> 00:19:18.160
He was able to show that it was indeed a distinct type of deer that could no longer be found alive.

00:19:18.160 --> 00:19:26.240
And so the Irish elk was one of the first animals to be recognised as being extinct.

00:19:26.240 --> 00:19:31.040
Georges Cuvier had solved the question of whether or not the Irish elk and moose

00:19:31.040 --> 00:19:33.920
were one and the same creature.

00:19:33.920 --> 00:19:37.600
But why did the Irish elk die out?

00:19:37.600 --> 00:19:43.520
Cuvier suggested that evolution had set it on a course of ever-increasing growth

00:19:43.520 --> 00:19:51.280
and that eventually the antlers became so large that the poor animal could not even lift its neck.

00:19:51.280 --> 00:19:56.720
He may not have been that far from the truth.

00:19:56.720 --> 00:20:02.720
It's now thought that the annual growth of the Irish elk antlers put a strain on their bodies.

00:20:02.720 --> 00:20:10.400
A significant proportion of minerals within their bones were extracted and moved into their growing antlers.

00:20:10.400 --> 00:20:15.760
This led to a seasonal osteoporosis with their bones weakening.

00:20:15.760 --> 00:20:23.440
They were, in effect, robbing one part of their body to boost another.

00:20:23.440 --> 00:20:27.680
It was a gamble that worked for thousands of years.

00:20:27.680 --> 00:20:33.280
But around 10,000 years ago, the climate began to warm.

00:20:33.280 --> 00:20:39.920
The nutrient-rich grasses that the elk relied upon began to disappear.

00:20:39.920 --> 00:20:48.160
Growing massive antlers may now have been too much of a drain and permanently weakened the skeleton.

00:20:48.160 --> 00:20:52.560
The change in diet may also have affected their ability to breed,

00:20:52.560 --> 00:20:58.880
with females no longer able to produce young every year.

00:20:58.880 --> 00:21:06.320
Whatever the reason, the Irish elk, with its magnificent antlers, finally vanished from the landscape.

00:21:06.320 --> 00:21:15.440
And in its place, the moose has become the largest deer on earth today.

00:21:15.440 --> 00:21:21.760
So while regeneration can give the salamander a second chance to a full life,

00:21:21.760 --> 00:21:27.680
the yearly regeneration of antlers in male moose is a risky strategy,

00:21:27.680 --> 00:21:36.880
but one with huge rewards for those with the best antlers.

00:21:57.680 --> 00:22:00.000
you
